Frontend Design
UI library
Use PrimeNG Community as the primary UI component library.
Prefer PrimeNG components whenever an equivalent exists.
Do not recreate standard UI components manually.
Customize PrimeNG through design tokens whenever possible.
Use Tailwind CSS for layout, spacing and responsive behavior.
Use Lucide Angular for icons.
Custom CSS
Keep custom CSS/SCSS to a minimum.
Do not write large amounts of bespoke CSS.
Do not duplicate PrimeNG component functionality with custom CSS.
Do not use inline styles unless technically necessary.
Prefer design tokens and Tailwind utilities over component-specific CSS.
Visual direction

The interface must look intentionally designed by an experienced product designer and frontend engineer.

The visual language should be:

professional
modern
restrained
distinctive
coherent
credible
practical

Visual quality must come primarily from:

typography
spacing
hierarchy
composition
alignment
contrast
consistency

Not from decorative effects.

Anti-AI design patterns

NEVER:

use generic AI/SaaS landing page patterns
use purple/blue gradients unless explicitly requested
use excessive glassmorphism
use excessive rounded cards
use huge hero sections
use decorative blobs
use random floating UI elements
use excessive shadows
use gradients purely as decoration
use excessive pill-shaped buttons
use excessive animations
use visual effects simply to make a page look "modern"
create repetitive card grids without a clear UX purpose
add decorative elements without a functional or compositional reason
create unnecessary sections to fill empty space
use generic AI-generated marketing copy
Design principles
Use a restrained typography hierarchy.
Use a limited color palette.
Maintain consistent spacing.
Maintain consistent border radius.
Maintain consistent interaction states.
Prioritize readability and usability.
Prefer whitespace over decoration.
Create clear primary, secondary and tertiary visual hierarchy.
Use asymmetry when it improves composition.
Do not make every element visually prominent.
Avoid visual noise.
Responsive design
Design mobile-first.
Support mobile, tablet, desktop and large desktop.
Prefer fluid layouts.
Avoid breakpoint-specific hacks.
Avoid hardcoded dimensions unless necessary.
Avoid separate desktop/mobile implementations unless absolutely necessary.